18
00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:56.800
I thought I was giving a

19
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.680
different session, but this is "Context is King" — over the last 30 minutes

20
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:01.440
so I've just been trying

21
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.280
to cram a 75-minute session into what's hopefully going to be 20 minutes.

22
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:06.400
So here we go.

23
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.280
Apologies if we go a little bit over, but this is a session:

24
00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:10.800
Context is King.

25
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.600
And I really want to kind of focus on the value

26
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.840
that context gives us when we're building with agents.

27
00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.240
Now, 2025 — last year — it was all about how we as companies

28
00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:27.080
can go as fast as we possibly can, or rather, as fast as we can adopt agents.

29
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.400
So how can we increase our usage of agents as much as possible?

30
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.000
This year it's all about: how can we use them effectively?

31
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:35.360
How can we get the most out of them?

32
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:37.200
How can we get value out of them?

33
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.520
And that ROI in terms of what we're spending?

34
00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.000
And this really comes down

35
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.040
to a number of things, but one of them is the model you choose.

36
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.560
So obviously tokens cost money.

37
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.280
Some are going to be better than others.

38
00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:52.360
But one thing that's better — as we've found out — than models,

39
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:56.320
or more important than models, is the agent that you choose.

40
00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.560
Because when we notice value that bumps up, it's not always the model

41
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.720
that's improving so much.

42
00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:04.760
It's typically the agent that rotates

43
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.440
and loops, getting the best out of the model.

44
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:10.200
But one thing above that:

45
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:13.560
when we actually identify, "oh, we're one-shotting this a ton more" —

46
00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.040
this is, you know, it's understanding what we want to do.

47
00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.560
It's really the quality of the context that really makes

48
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.080
the model sing and really, really work well.

49
00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.200
So context, for me, is how you get the most

50
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.280
out of your agent, and getting the most out of your agent —

51
00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.600
or rather, using an agent — is how you get the most out of your model.

52
00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:33.640
So these three things

53
00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:37.320
in combination will get you to that real value.

54
00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.520
Let me give you a quick example.

55
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.360
This is something I did a little while back, actually.

56
00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:42.760
So numbers may change,

57
00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:47.120
but here you can see I'm actually running some tests against a skill.

58
00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:51.520
And in this case the skill is the "first to five best practices" skill.

59
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.080
And you can see I'm using several models with several agents here:

60
00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.800
Opus, Sonnet, and Haiku.

61
00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.600
And you can see the scores that they get in terms of how good

62
00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.400
every single model is performing on certain tasks.

63
00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.000
And you can see — yes, you would expect Opus to do well —

64
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.440
but with the skill there's actually not as much of a difference between them.

65
00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:16.800
And when you think about the cost that is incurred with these,

66
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:22.280
the actual value, the ROI, is actually far, far smaller —

67
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:25.960
the difference between these models —

68
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:29.160
once you've started using context and using skills.

69
00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:31.800
Okay.

70
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.800
So we've talked about context a little bit,

71
00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.800
but what do we mean by context?

72
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.680
This is an important question.

73
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.760
Well, anything that the agent can really access — whether that's in its memory,

74
00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.080
whether it's the files that are local to the system,

75
00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.120
whether it's things that we load in like skills and rules,

76
00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.200
whether it's documentation around, you know, third-party libraries

77
00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:57.960
or our own proprietary libraries that we create and provide to it.

78
00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.880
It could be even our messages —

79
00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.800
all that adds up as context for the agent — and test outputs,

80
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.880
whether it's linters or whatever that gets run and passed back in.

81
00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:10.760
All of this becomes context.

82
00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:14.000
And then we'll dig into a couple.

83
00:04:14.120 --> 00:04:16.160
The first one we're going to look at is rules,

84
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.040
and how you can use rules within your agent as context.

85
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.200
Now, rules are essentially constraints — behavioural constraints —

86
00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.440
that an agent will always read.

87
00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:30.040
Typically these tend to be in something like —

88
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:34.120
not sure if I've got this — something like a CLAUDE.md:

89
00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.240
things that the agent will always go through.

90
00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:41.280
These are things that you should always do, not just based on

91
00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:44.440
what the user is asking — but you should always do these things.

92
00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:47.440
Very often: organisational rules and things like that.

93
00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.360
Skills, on the other hand,

94
00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:55.720
are much more dependent on the actual task or the act that the agent's doing.

95
00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:58.080
So they're kind of like — you can think of them a little bit

96
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.560
more like callable procedures. When you need them,

97
00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.400
you call them, you invoke them.

98
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:04.480
They're not always fully read.

99
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.960
They're disclosed progressively,

100
00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.440
as and when the agent feels like it wants to use that thing.

101
00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:12.560
There are a couple of reasons why

102
00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:14.320
sometimes it will or won't use it.

103
00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.320
If it thinks it can do something itself, it may not actually use

104
00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.440
the skill. But we'll look at that in a little bit.

105
00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:28.080
So the practical distinction is: rules are passively run.

106
00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.080
They're always there, and they shape everything the agent does.

107
00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.160
A skill is triggered for that specific job.

108
00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:40.080
When we look at a SKILL.md, we'll dig a little bit deeper into skills.

109
00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:43.480
Now there are really two pieces of this: metadata at the very top,

110
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.160
which are mandatory —

111
00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:46.800
the name and the description.

112
00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.880
And the description, of course, is super important, because that's —

113
00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:53.440
well, the name and the description are all that

114
00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:58.560
the agent has to load in order to know whether it activates it or not.

115
00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.800
And an activation of a skill is an agent saying, "Look,

116
00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:06.400
I think I need more information in this skill to perform this task.

117
00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.560
I will read more, and I will load more of that skill into context."

118
00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.800
And it can choose how much of that skill it does load into context based

119
00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.000
on the task.

120
00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:19.600
So when we

121
00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:22.680
think about skill activation, it goes from knowing

122
00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.480
nothing to knowing exactly what it needs — like in an instant.

123
00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:27.960
And that's the key.

124
00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:33.280
So the way it does this is by loading things into the context window.

125
00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.360
Now the context window is essentially a finite piece of memory —

126
00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.080
a finite area that contains a number of everything that I said:

127
00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:47.040
files in the file system, skills, maybe project files or user files.

128
00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:49.600
And there's absolutely a Goldilocks zone.

129
00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:54.360
Don't be thinking that area —

130
00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:58.080
that context window — I can fill it completely

131
00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.080
and an agent will work

132
00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.080
just as well with a full context window as with a small context window.

133
00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:09.200
The Goldilocks zone is: if you provide too little context for a task,

134
00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:13.520
it will perform the task, but it may not do it the way you wish

135
00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.320
that task to be carried out.

136
00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:19.240
So maybe it makes decisions that you'd actually prefer

137
00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:22.240
things being done in a different way.

138
00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:25.600
When there's too much context, it overwhelms the agent.

139
00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:29.400
And when you overwhelm the agent, you effectively degrade

140
00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.560
the performance, because it's actually missing key pieces of information.

141
00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.560
So what you want is the Goldilocks zone — this "just right" zone.

142
00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:36.840
If you haven't heard of the Goldilocks zone

143
00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:40.880
— obviously there's the Three Bears — but I'd love to

144
00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:44.720
think of it as the distance from a star

145
00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:46.920
at which a planet has to be to actually hold life.

146
00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:48.080
Too close: it's too hot.

147
00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.000
Too far: the bacteria, etc., etc.,

148
00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:53.320
won't grow. So that's the Goldilocks zone.

149
00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:57.320
So you can always see your context state — context include code, for example — and you'll see

150
00:07:57.320 --> 00:08:02.080
how your context window is filled.

151
00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:03.400
And like I say,

152
00:08:03.400 --> 00:08:05.040
there are a bunch of different window sizes.

153
00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:08.080
But don't be fooled into thinking you can completely

154
00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.960
fill that window without the performance degrading.

155
00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:14.360
Okay.

156
00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:17.760
You can also — just as well as adding things to the window —

157
00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:21.160
you can obviously free up those tokens, and you can do that through compaction.

158
00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.040
It happens automatically — using things like, well,

159
00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.280
when you fill up your context window you'll get automatic compaction.

160
00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:31.720
One thing that people don't always know is you can compact very often

161
00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:36.080
with instructions afterwards — to say, when you do it manually, compact.

162
00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.440
"I want to do this thing afterwards," and it will summarise

163
00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:42.680
based on knowledge of what you are trying to achieve next.

164
00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:46.120
So it'll keep things in the context window so that it doesn't

165
00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:49.240
have to reload anything else in, because it knows

166
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.360
enough of what to do next.

167
00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:53.560
Cool.

168
00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:56.760
I'm going to do a skills demo, and then we're going to jump through

169
00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:59.920
potential problems that we as an organisation

170
00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:03.720
may face when doing this more at scale.

171
00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:08.200
So as part of this skills demo, what I'm going to do is actually

172
00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:12.320
show you an app that I've been working on over the last couple of days,

173
00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:17.800
which is called "Talk to Skill."

174
00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:19.880
Oh cool, it's just finished. That's awesome.

175
00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:24.400
Talk to Skill is an application that uses a skill itself.

176
00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:28.440
But we're going to be running this live at Dev Con —

177
00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.240
the conference that was just mentioned.

178
00:09:30.240 --> 00:09:31.080
And what we're going to do

179
00:09:31.080 --> 00:09:35.280
is turn every single talk, every single session, into a skill.

180
00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:37.000
Why would you want to do that?

181
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.000
Well, maybe you want to ask some Q&amp;A of

182
00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:45.160
that speaker — currently, that speaker — maybe you want to learn

183
00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:49.040
or bounce some ideas off someone, or say, "How do I do this with my project?"

184
00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:52.920
And you can use that skill to get good advice from the lessons

185
00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:57.880
learned in the session with your scenario.

186
00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:02.200
So in this case, just today, Guy — our founder —

187
00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:06.280
practically demoed his session, the keynote session,

188
00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:10.160
at our weekly lunch and learn at lunchtime.

189
00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:15.840
Now, what this does is it actually takes from a Granola folder.

190
00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:20.840
So every single time a Granola transcript is captured, it throws it into a folder.

191
00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:25.880
And when we recognise that — this refreshes every 30 seconds or so for now —

192
00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:29.240
when we recognise something new comes in, we have a skill

193
00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:34.280
that automatically creates a session skill and publishes it across to —

194
00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:38.280
then it does a number of other steps, which I'll go into in just a second.

195
00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:40.760
But here I can see — if I come here —

196
00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:43.080
here I can see we have the full transcript.

197
00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.360
We have a bunch of quotes.

198
00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:47.360
We have a tile JSON.

199
00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:52.760
So this is the version of the skill which we've uploaded into Tessl,

200
00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:55.920
and an outline that describes, you know,

201
00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:59.960
essentially what the session is about.

202
00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.000
And this SKILL.md, which has a name, it has a description,

203
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:07.320
and some other things — how to use this skill, and so forth.

204
00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:12.560
Now, if I come over here, you will see I have this

205
00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:16.000
skill that exists. I can install it —

206
00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:20.360
so I can run npm install — and I'll do that in just a second.

207
00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:22.400
And information

208
00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:27.160
about how to use this skill and the kinds of things that I should do.

209
00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:29.680
So let's go ahead and actually play with this.

210
00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:35.480
So if I was to run a test install — or in fact, let's just

211
00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:40.280
copy and paste and use that straight out of the box — I've now installed

212
00:11:40.280 --> 00:11:45.360
that tile or that plugin, which contains the skill, into my environment.

213
00:11:45.400 --> 00:11:48.400
Now, if I run Claude and say,

214
00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:52.640
"Tell me about thinking start" —

215
00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:54.720
tell me why

216
00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:57.720
I should

217
00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.720
consider

218
00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:06.880
context to be like code —

219
00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:12.040
let's see if it auto-activates my skill.

220
00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.080
There we go. It's activated that skill.

221
00:12:15.080 --> 00:12:16.560
It reads the outline.

222
00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:17.720
It's obviously doing some work

223
00:12:17.720 --> 00:12:21.520
and actually loading the relevant pieces into my context.

224
00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.080
And it should hopefully — depending on

225
00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:25.240
how Claude is today, who's still using Claude?

226
00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:28.320
I've heard so many people switching — who switched

227
00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:31.320
from Claude to Cursor in the last two weeks.

228
00:12:31.720 --> 00:12:34.960
Last month, a few people — three people.

229
00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:35.920
Very, very interesting.

230
00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:38.840
I was just talking with Alan in the office just now.

231
00:12:38.840 --> 00:12:39.600
I was saying,

232
00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:43.120
"My God, I just asked it to create me a very simple skill for this demo.

233
00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:47.040
And it's taken three minutes to create a pirate skill. Like,

234
00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:48.320
why is it taking so long?"

235
00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:51.920
And I think it's just the deep thinking that's just taking — it's just crazy.

236
00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:53.880
Anyway, where was I?

237
00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:55.840
Okay.

238
00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:57.320
Skills are already behaving like code.

239
00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:00.600
It gives me quotes, it gives me more information.

240
00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:04.800
And now I might actually say, "Okay, what do I do to this skill?

241
00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:06.000
What do I do to this skill

242
00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.040
to treat it like code?" It will probably say, "Okay, you need to version it.

243
00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:10.840
You need to do these various things."

244
00:13:10.840 --> 00:13:12.960
I might say, "Tell me" —

245
00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:15.200
he talks about the — he talks about a CDLC.

246
00:13:15.200 --> 00:13:16.960
I can see it there.

247
00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:18.360
There's the clock.

248
00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:20.800
"Tell me about

249
00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:25.000
the CDLC," and so forth.

250
00:13:25.080 --> 00:13:28.080
And I can now ask questions and get clarification.

251
00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.120
This is hopefully — fingers crossed —

252
00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:33.120
like I'm talking with the speaker, using those quotes

253
00:13:33.120 --> 00:13:36.120
and potentially other information from outside as well.

254
00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:38.360
Okay.

255
00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:41.600
So this is going to be available for every single session, live,

256
00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:46.280
that we do at Dev Con — a little bit of fun. A couple of other things.

257
00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:49.840
What I — when we do this within Tessl —

258
00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:53.640
the first thing — in fact, let me go back to the app.

259
00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:57.880
The first thing I do: we create the skill and we publish it

260
00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:58.760
to the registry.

261
00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:01.360
If I go to the history, I added this just today.

262
00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:04.360
I build the skill

263
00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:08.000
and I publish it to the registry as version 1.0.1.

264
00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:10.080
Then I review the skill, because

265
00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:13.920
one of the big questions is: how do we know if this skill is any good?

266
00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:15.600
So I review it.

267
00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:20.440
And that basically checks if the skill meets the best practices —

268
00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:24.240
the quality that, you know, we have seen from

269
00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:29.240
Anthropic best practices — I think not Codex — best practices

270
00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.600
that they have published — and it validates: is this similar to those

271
00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:36.000
best practices? It makes those changes.

272
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:39.520
Then it performs optimisation.

273
00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:45.480
And optimisation is really about the impact that this skill makes.

274
00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:47.760
In other words: if I was to ask this question

275
00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:51.920
without the skill, would the agent alone be able to provide me

276
00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.600
with a good enough answer?

277
00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:56.840
So we run the same tests with and without,

278
00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:00.840
and we identify how much of an impact that is.

279
00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:03.880
So we do all of that, and then we can provide that.

280
00:15:03.880 --> 00:15:07.840
If I go back to the Tessl registry, you can see right now

281
00:15:07.960 --> 00:15:12.920
this is 1.3x times better than without the skill.

282
00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:16.080
You can see the quality has gone up to 90% from

283
00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:19.440
my original version — from my original 82%.

284
00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.840
So we're identifying what are the best practices we're missing,

285
00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:26.120
and then how can we update with those best practices?

286
00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:29.880
Okay, I'll mention —

287
00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:32.880
actually, we also run a security scan on this skill.

288
00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.920
This is Tessl, but this is not Tessl.

289
00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:36.040
I used to run at Snyk.

290
00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:38.960
I used to — I used to work at Snyk for like six years.

291
00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:41.440
I keep referring to Tessl as Snyk.

292
00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:43.800
I'm just starting, after two years at Tessl,

293
00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:47.840
to refer to Snyk as Tessl now, so I think it's balancing out.

294
00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:51.840
But this is a security report — a security scan run by Snyk —

295
00:15:51.880 --> 00:15:55.320
trying to identify it using LLM as a judge here, trying to identify

296
00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:59.640
potential malicious issues or bad security practices within my skill.

297
00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:04.680
And it will provide me — if I go here to my security — it will provide me

298
00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:09.560
with any security issues that could exist there in my evals.

299
00:16:09.640 --> 00:16:12.000
Oh, why is that?

300
00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:14.280
I need to go to my correct version.

301
00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:18.040
In my evals, you can see where things are good

302
00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:21.080
and where things are bad, and in some cases where things

303
00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:26.440
actually regress — where my context confuses the agent.

304
00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:29.680
And so this gives us insight into what the context

305
00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:33.280
is doing a good job at, and where we need to actually improve.

306
00:16:35.240 --> 00:16:37.680
Okay, let's jump back to

307
00:16:37.680 --> 00:16:41.200
slides — and I'll — where's my slides? — here.

308
00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:44.400
And I'll flip through a couple of additional.

309
00:16:47.640 --> 00:16:48.520
Why you're not working —

310
00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:49.320
there we go.

311
00:16:49.320 --> 00:16:52.600
And I'll flip through a couple of important things

312
00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:56.480
that we should be considering as we start scaling our usage of skills —

313
00:16:56.480 --> 00:17:00.440
or really even as an organisation or as an individual.

314
00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:03.560
So here are some scenarios.

315
00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:06.360
I've got six scenarios that we need to think about.

316
00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:08.480
Whether we use Tessl or something else,

317
00:17:08.480 --> 00:17:13.680
we should have a mechanism in place where we know how we can achieve these things.

318
00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:15.160
Your team or company, right?

319
00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:16.240
Hundreds of skills.

320
00:17:16.240 --> 00:17:18.320
They're not — but they're local.

321
00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:21.480
Essentially, each developer is writing them locally and using them locally.

322
00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.720
They're not discoverable by others.

323
00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:28.160
How do we unlock the value that one individual gets, and effectively

324
00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:31.280
multiply that across a team or across an organisation?

325
00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:34.160
So a really important way — we need some registry.

326
00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:37.840
We need some mechanism where others can find those types of skills.

327
00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:40.680
Your team installs loads of skills.

328
00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:44.520
Every developer has a different set of skills developing on the same project.

329
00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:47.600
How do you achieve consistency across the whole project?

330
00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:50.520
So maybe your organisation says, "You know what?

331
00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:53.400
We need these skills always to be on" — always-on

332
00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:56.560
skills across every single team in our organisation.

333
00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:59.040
That's important to mandate.

334
00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:03.400
Also, if I have a set of skills that I want everyone to use as a project —

335
00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:07.160
maybe it uses third-party library dependencies, maybe it needs to use

336
00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:12.600
a proprietary context for a proprietary library that I've developed.

337
00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:15.200
That context should be available to everyone.

338
00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:18.320
Otherwise, imagine lots of developers

339
00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:21.960
on a team, all using different levels of knowledge.

340
00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:23.960
Some people don't even know about proprietary libraries.

341
00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:25.640
Some people do. You just wouldn't have it.

342
00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:29.200
You'd constantly have pull request reviews saying, "You have to use this."

343
00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:31.800
We don't want to do it to people. We don't want to do it to agents, either.

344
00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:34.720
I've got loads of skills installed.

345
00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:36.520
Are they being used correctly?

346
00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:40.360
Activation is a key, key problem, and I'll talk about that

347
00:18:40.360 --> 00:18:42.040
in just a second, actually.

348
00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:45.200
But how do I tell if my agent is actually activating?

349
00:18:45.200 --> 00:18:46.960
That's such a key, key piece.

350
00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:47.880
Because there's no point

351
00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:52.400
getting our skills to be really good if we actually don't use them in practice.

352
00:18:52.680 --> 00:18:56.200
Dev teams install skills from everywhere.

353
00:18:56.240 --> 00:18:57.200
How do we audit

354
00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:01.040
and ensure we're not using poor security skills or malicious skills?

355
00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:05.480
This is all about the scanning — whether via Snyk directly or through a registry.

356
00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:10.000
So, so important from an audit point of view. We haven't yet hit that

357
00:19:10.120 --> 00:19:12.800
"Oh shit, someone's deployed a malicious skill

358
00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:16.040
and it's really having problems in a production environment."

359
00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:19.040
It's a headline waiting to happen.

360
00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:20.800
This is a key one.

361
00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:23.040
You're after a specific skill.

362
00:19:23.040 --> 00:19:25.760
You know, there are five developers who've all created the same skill.

363
00:19:25.760 --> 00:19:28.600
There's probably 20 more who have created other skills.

364
00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:30.120
Which one do you use?

365
00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:31.600
Which one is malicious?

366
00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:33.240
Which one is the highest quality?

367
00:19:33.240 --> 00:19:35.080
Which one has the biggest impact?

368
00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:38.840
You need to be able to understand which are the better skills,

369
00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:41.680
and you need to be able to track where there are multiple skills

370
00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:45.240
and how you actually remove those skills from view.

371
00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:47.280
And finally, where you do

372
00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:51.560
get a bunch of skills — typically what you'll do is you'll get stale states,

373
00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:55.040
whereby things are moving on, certain things are stale.

374
00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:56.440
How do you keep that up to date?

375
00:19:56.440 --> 00:20:00.320
And this is very much thinking about context as code.

376
00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:04.880
You want to maintain that — as your code changes, your context changes.

377
00:20:04.920 --> 00:20:08.360
As models change, your context will need to change,

378
00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:12.520
because models will learn more, they'll know better, and your context

379
00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:15.280
will maybe need to remove things because the agents are good.

380
00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:19.120
So those are some things about how we scale.

381
00:20:19.120 --> 00:20:22.840
The last thing I want to talk about — and I'll do this in just a few minutes — is

382
00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:27.000
how, when we are building skills, there are things that we should think about when

383
00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:30.360
we actually create the skills ourselves and actually write the skills.

384
00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:34.160
And we've learned this because — you know, in fact —

385
00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:38.200
when we first gave this presentation, AI Engineer in London —

386
00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:41.560
this was the rate of skill growth in 14 weeks.

387
00:20:41.560 --> 00:20:45.680
We went from 12 skills in GitHub in November

388
00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:49.360
up to, you know, 5,500 skills in just 14 weeks.

389
00:20:49.360 --> 00:20:51.040
And it's probably way beyond that.

390
00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:54.040
We could probably refresh that number now.

391
00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:57.320
The key stat is: once a skill gets

392
00:20:57.360 --> 00:21:01.080
added into GitHub, nine times out of ten it never gets updated.

393
00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:03.560
What are the chances of that skill being perfect out of the box?

394
00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:05.760
Virtually zero, right?

395
00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:06.880
So what do we do about it?

396
00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:10.560
Well, we decided to go on a mass pull request submission,

397
00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:12.440
which I guess some people loved.

398
00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:15.920
Some people didn't love quite as much, but we were trying to help.

399
00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:19.280
And so we raised 622 pull requests.

400
00:21:19.360 --> 00:21:21.520
And we essentially kind of said, "Look,

401
00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:25.600
there's a whole bunch of things that we know are wrong with your skill.

402
00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:29.600
Can we send a pull request to try and make suggestions to help?"

403
00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:33.680
And from that we learnt what are the key things

404
00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:37.200
that most people are failing at in their skill creation.

405
00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:41.000
The first one — and this is probably the biggest problem

406
00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:44.800
that developers generally have — is thinking,

407
00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:48.280
"If I just write a crappy description because it's a description,

408
00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:51.760
I'll get away with it and I don't actually need it."

409
00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:53.720
But this is actually really, really important.

410
00:21:53.720 --> 00:21:57.200
And there are a number of things that are important within the description as well.

411
00:21:57.200 --> 00:22:02.760
If you can make that description specific to maybe tools that you use

412
00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:07.920
or technology that you use, the agent has a higher chance of using it,

413
00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:13.960
because it has less in its memory and in its training data

414
00:22:14.000 --> 00:22:17.320
about this specific tool or this specific use case.

415
00:22:17.320 --> 00:22:21.800
So it's more likely to inject the skill context into that.

416
00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:27.240
So before, we very often see, you know, "A helpful skill for code review and quality

417
00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:30.840
improvement" — an agent knows how to do that already, or it thinks it does.

418
00:22:30.840 --> 00:22:34.440
But after, you're talking about running ESLint with project rules —

419
00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:38.120
you know, this is very specific, and it gets triggered much, much more.

420
00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:41.840
Here's quite a shocking stat.

421
00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:48.120
Only 40% — 41% of times — will a skill activate

422
00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:51.480
if an agent isn't forced to use a skill. And by "forced

423
00:22:51.480 --> 00:22:54.640
to use a skill," we're talking about using a slash command in a task.

424
00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:59.280
So if you just say, "I want to do this thing" and a skill exists,

425
00:22:59.520 --> 00:23:02.640
will that agent actually activate that skill to do it, or will it

426
00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:04.840
try and do it itself? 41% won't.

427
00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:06.320
And there are many reasons.

428
00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:09.520
One of which is "god skills" — a skill

429
00:23:09.520 --> 00:23:13.600
that tries to do everything: the one all-encompassing skill.

430
00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:14.640
And there are a few reasons

431
00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:19.520
why this can fail — particularly with an activation one — because,

432
00:23:19.560 --> 00:23:23.920
well, actually, if there are 50 things and I have a specific

433
00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:28.320
task, it might well activate a completely different skill in my plugin.

434
00:23:28.320 --> 00:23:32.080
There's just too much overlap between a number of skills.

435
00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:35.360
It'll either do that, or it just won't activate at all.

436
00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:38.560
Context bloat is another one,

437
00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:43.000
and this is where you actually put so much context into your skill

438
00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:47.080
that the agent won't actually pull the right pieces from it, typically.

439
00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:50.960
And this is actually a best practice with Anthropic and so forth.

440
00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:54.880
They talk about keeping your SKILL.md below

441
00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:57.800
500 lines. Optimised skills,

442
00:23:57.800 --> 00:24:01.440
interestingly, use 40% fewer tokens

443
00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:04.480
and complete tasks in half the time.

444
00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:08.320
And this is about either removing duplication or removing

445
00:24:08.320 --> 00:24:12.240
things that the agent most likely knows how to do anyway.

446
00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:14.760
And so all of this is about running evals

447
00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:17.760
and making sure your skill is all it can be.

448
00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:18.960
And that's everything.

449
00:24:18.960 --> 00:24:23.480
I hope that wasn't too long, but there's a ton more in this type of

450
00:24:23.480 --> 00:24:23.920
session.

451
00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:27.280
And perhaps I can share with you a full deck afterwards as well.

452
00:24:27.760 --> 00:24:30.320
I've got time for questions.

453
00:24:30.320 --> 00:24:32.080
Yeah, a little bit, a little bit.

454
00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:33.640
A very short question.

455
00:24:33.640 --> 00:24:36.560
Anyone have any questions or comments? Yes.

456
00:24:36.560 --> 00:24:38.360
Do we have a queue over there?

457
00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:45.080
My question is, you said 41%. Have you

458
00:24:45.080 --> 00:24:47.920
tried anything like force skill usage?

459
00:24:47.920 --> 00:24:54.520
I remember — for Anthropic ones you can use hooks to say, like, every turn,

460
00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:57.520
basically go check if you need to use a skill, or anything like that.

461
00:24:57.560 --> 00:25:00.000
Or is there anything else?

462
00:25:00.000 --> 00:25:00.320
Yeah.

463
00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:04.800
So I mean, you can — the best way to force it —

464
00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:09.480
well, even if you use a hook and there are 100 skills that you have

465
00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:14.080
installed, the hook then still has to determine what the best skill to use is.

466
00:25:14.120 --> 00:25:18.120
And it might be that it's just not used the right skill, because it's

467
00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:21.760
way too complicated to work out which the right skill is.

468
00:25:21.800 --> 00:25:24.800
So I think you could use hooks,

469
00:25:24.800 --> 00:25:28.360
you could enforce use of a skill by using a slash command.

470
00:25:28.400 --> 00:25:32.560
But I think the biggest value,

471
00:25:32.560 --> 00:25:36.160
the biggest bang for your buck, is fixing the description

472
00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:39.160
and actually fixing the way you bundle skills together.

473
00:25:39.160 --> 00:25:42.240
So breaking them up to make sure you haven't got so many skills

474
00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:46.760
that it's just confusing — or, you know, bringing smaller skills together

475
00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:51.360
so that when you have a number of things

476
00:25:51.360 --> 00:25:54.200
that you always want to do together, maybe that actually fits

477
00:25:54.200 --> 00:25:56.400
in a single skill.

478
00:25:56.400 --> 00:25:59.440
But I would say — you know —

479
00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:02.640
you can't improve on something you can't measure.

480
00:26:02.640 --> 00:26:05.920
So I think the biggest key is: run evaluations.

481
00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:09.000
So we — as part of our new skill optimiser —

482
00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:10.920
we run activation tests as well.

483
00:26:10.920 --> 00:26:14.880
And we have insights that show when your skills are getting activated as well.

484
00:26:14.920 --> 00:26:16.360
So I would say: make sure

485
00:26:16.360 --> 00:26:20.640
you have a plan for measurement, and then try to improve that.

486
00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:22.520
That could be hooks, but I think there are

487
00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:26.200
low-hanging fruit first, and then hooks afterwards to say, "Hey,

488
00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:29.560
could you have improved this with a skill, and which skill would it be?"

489
00:26:29.600 --> 00:26:34.440
Maybe you have a list of skills to use, but you're almost duplicating work there.

490
00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:36.120
Yeah. Thank you. Cool.

491
00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:38.800
Perhaps just one more. Yeah.

492
00:26:38.800 --> 00:26:39.320
Gentleman, just —

493
00:26:45.200 --> 00:26:45.880
Higher.

494
00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:48.200
Wait for the presentation.

495
00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:51.120
What about — have you thought about, let's say, conflict?

496
00:26:51.120 --> 00:26:53.840
So let's say if you have an element model —

497
00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:54.360
okay —

498
00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:55.840
as you know that

499
00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:59.600
if — let's say we talk about humans — let's say you have two concepts,

500
00:26:59.800 --> 00:27:03.200
but they're conflicting between those two models.

501
00:27:03.200 --> 00:27:03.560
Yeah.

502
00:27:03.560 --> 00:27:07.640
I mean, two thoughts — let's say in the beginning, then we would actually

503
00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:13.000
lay them out on a piece of paper and then basically do a comparison of sorts, right?

504
00:27:13.000 --> 00:27:13.560
Right.

505
00:27:13.560 --> 00:27:17.080
So if I do that, that happened — that that happened.

506
00:27:17.320 --> 00:27:20.880
But with an LLM, I think — do you think that

507
00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:24.480
that can be a huge conflict over —

508
00:27:24.480 --> 00:27:28.320
either conflict of interest or conflict between two skills at the

509
00:27:28.320 --> 00:27:29.680
same time? I absolutely think so.

510
00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:32.960
And I think it gets to the stage where it's almost inevitable,

511
00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:37.360
once you start adding lots and lots of skills to your environment.

512
00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:41.560
It's one thing that we are looking to add into our platform

513
00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:44.920
for the more enterprise-style deployments,

514
00:27:44.920 --> 00:27:48.440
because they will have — we think they will have — so many skills.

515
00:27:48.440 --> 00:27:50.840
What this needs to be is — it's

516
00:27:50.840 --> 00:27:55.040
a hard thing to test, first of all, because you can't test them in isolation.

517
00:27:55.040 --> 00:27:58.280
It'll typically be when you install 50 skills

518
00:27:58.280 --> 00:28:02.160
from so many different places that all of a sudden you have two people

519
00:28:02.160 --> 00:28:06.600
with differing opinions, and it has to pick one over the other.

520
00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:08.640
Or weirdly, it merges the two,

521
00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:11.320
and actually you're not satisfying either skill.

522
00:28:11.320 --> 00:28:15.320
So what you then need is two things: first of all,

523
00:28:15.320 --> 00:28:19.400
conflict detection — to be able to say, "Okay, you've got two skills here that

524
00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:22.520
have a difference of opinion."

525
00:28:22.520 --> 00:28:24.440
Or even in similarity sometimes.

526
00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:26.120
Yeah, it could be. Yeah.

527
00:28:26.120 --> 00:28:28.120
But then you need a merge resolution.

528
00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:32.000
And so it's like, "Okay, which one should win?"

529
00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:36.960
And it's like — maybe the agent should — you can describe something with the agent.

530
00:28:36.960 --> 00:28:38.640
But more likely what it is:

531
00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:42.600
you need a path to remove that conflict.

532
00:28:42.600 --> 00:28:46.920
So you need to either start removing a skill or adapting a skill

533
00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:49.080
so that it removes the conflict.

534
00:28:49.080 --> 00:28:52.920
But I think we're at the earliest stage

535
00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:55.200
where people are just starting to throw skills in.

536
00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:59.280
It's something that's bound to happen, and we can only kick the can down the road

537
00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:03.120
a little bit before it truly happens at a larger scale, for sure.

